{"networkAttributes":[{"KEGG_PATHWAY_IMAGE":"https://www.kegg.jp/kegg/pathway/spo/spo00260.png","KEGG_PATHWAY_ORG":"spo","modules_id":["Threonine biosynthesis, aspartate => homoserine => threonine [PATH:spo00260]","Serine biosynthesis, glycerate-3P => serine [PATH:spo00260]","Glycine cleavage system [PATH:spo00260]"],"KEGG_PATHWAY_ID":"path:spo00260","classes":["Metabolism","Amino acid metabolism"],"description":"Serine is derived from 3-phospho-D-glycerate, an intermediate of glycolysis [MD:M00020], and glycine is derived from serine. Threonine is an essential amino acid, which animals cannot synthesize. In bacteria and plants, threonine is derived from aspartate [MD:M00018].","diseases":[""],"diseases_id":[""],"KEGG_PATHWAY_LINK":"https://www.kegg.jp/kegg-bin/show_pathway?spo00260","modules":["spo_M00018","spo_M00020","spo_M00621"],"name":"Glycine, serine and threonine metabolism [spo00260]_1"}]},
